steps.body:
When input has several values on separate lines, read all of stdin into `input` with `require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8")`, then split the trimmed text on `"\n"` to get an array of lines.

```stdin
Ada
36
```

```javascript harness
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
{{code}}
console.log(lines);
```

```javascript
const lines = input.trim().split("\n"); // ["Ada", "36"]
```

Trim first. Input usually ends with a newline, and splitting without trimming leaves an empty string as the last line.

When you know how many lines there are, destructure them into named variables in one step. Each one is still text, so convert the numeric ones.

```stdin
Ada
36
```

```javascript harness
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const lines = input.trim().split("\n");
{{code}}
console.log(name, age);
```

```javascript
const [name, ageText] = lines;
const age = Number(ageText);
```

A line keeps everything on it, spaces included, so a value like `Blue Pen` stays in one piece. Splitting on lines only cuts at the line breaks.

example.code:
const input = require("fs").readFileSync(0, "utf8");
const lines = input.trim().split("\n");
const [name, ageText] = lines;
const age = Number(ageText);

console.log(`Read ${lines.length} lines`);
console.log(`${name} is ${age}, ${100 - age} years until 100`);

